A||||11||gtsonly||tires and sizes||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||05-19-2000||06:44 AM||||i have read you can go as big as a 245 size tire in the rear and a 225 in the front. are you still able to to go to those nice and wide tires with lower springs? whats a good tire to get in those sizes?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mshill||05-19-2000||10:11 AM||||I know someone has gone over this before as well, but what width rims do you need with those 245's in the back and 225's in the front.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-19-2000||10:51 AM||||I have lowered my car and it has 235/60R14's on it all the way around. I will be going to 245/50r15's which is a smaller tire per overall dimesions. The 235/60R14's rub alittle at lock.<P><BR>Tire size_	Sidewall_	Radius_	Diam_	Circum_	Revolutions_	Difference<BR>215/60-14_	5.1 in_	12.1 in_	24.2 in_	75.9 in_	835 / mile_	0.00%<BR>225/50-15_	4.4 in_	11.9 in_	23.9 in_	75.0 in_	845 / mile_	-1.20%<BR>205/55-15_	4.4 in_	11.9 in_	23.9 in_	75.0 in_	845 / mile_	-1.20%<BR>225/55-16_	4.9 in_	12.9 in_	25.7 in_	80.9 in_	783 / mile_	6.60%<BR>225/50-16_	4.4 in_	12.4 in_	24.9 in_	78.1 in_	811 / mile_	2.90%<BR>245/50-15_	4.8 in_	12.3 in_	24.6 in_	77.4 in_	818 / mile_	2.00%<BR>235/60-14_	5.6 in_	12.6 in_	25.1 in_	78.9 in_	803 / mile_	3.90%<P><BR>The 215/60r14's were the original tire size. This gives overall sizes and speedo errors. The Rim sizes for a 15" need to be 7" wide to hold a 245 series tire.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||lennyb4||05-19-2000||11:23 AM||||I have BFG's 225/70/14 all the way around and they work real good. The only problem i have is if i turn to sharp coming out of my driveway they rub alittle (driveways got a slope to it).||||reg||1
Z||000004||jhogans||05-19-2000||12:15 PM||||I have the GT Lace rims on my 86 GT.  What are the largest tires I can go with for the front and rear.  Thanks for any info.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-19-2000||12:31 PM||||The 225/70R14's are too big for the fiero. your sidewall is too high so it will hamper your handling and your spedometer error is 6.2%. (see below) If you want to improve your handling just go to a 60 series tire.<P>225/70-14_6.2 in_13.2 in_26.4 in_82.9 in_764 / mile_6.2%  <P>The biggest tire overall dimensions so that the tire doesn't rub for a non-lowered Fiero is 13" wide 25.8" high and a circumference of 81.5" If it's lowered you need to stay under 12.5" wide, 25.0 inches tall and 78.6" in circumference. The higher the sidewall the more the tire will roll under during hard cornering.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||05-19-2000||12:53 PM||||Remember...the wheel offset is very important as to what sizes tires you can use, in the rear you will have the strut to deal with and in the front where the rim edge is in relation to lower A frame at lock. I have a Ferrari replica and to fill the wheel openings and give the car correct stance, i have custom made wheels with 205/60/16 front and 225/60/16 rear. At 75mph my speedo read just a tad under 65mph. The speedo dial face from FOCA that has 200mph works perfectly without modifing the circuit board. Just another note...if u do have oversize tires and yourspeedo is off and u have access to a portable GPS you can correct the stock speedo error by simply removing the faceplate and pulling the needle straight out and off and moving it to point to correct speed. cheap trick but it works. It may seem that your going to break something, some needles are very tite, a light push onto shaft is plenty when you get it where you want it,||||reg||1
Z||000007||lennyb4||05-19-2000||01:12 PM||||Thanks guys for the info. The only bad part is i just got the tires around 1+1/2 months ago. I was planning on lowering it next spring. I guess i like them because they fill the wheel well real nice. And i have noticed a differance in sharp cornering. Will it hurt anything if i leave them on for now? I would like to keep them until winter when i put the car up and start on the full restoration.||||reg||14
Z||000008||gtsonly||05-19-2000||04:12 PM||||my 86 gt has 15x7 rims. i am told on another site that you can go to a 245/60-15 on the rear and a 225/60/15 on the front with no problems at all. thats why the fiero has two different size tires. big in the rear and smaller in front. helps with under/oversteer. the site said by going larger(245's) in the rear helps with handling. same on the front. but can you got that set-up and still lower the car? i like the look of the wheel well filled with rubber||||reg||14
Z||000009||1FST2M6||05-19-2000||11:21 PM||||there is a term  "point of diminishing return"  with a 245 on a 7" wheel you have 1.3" of the tire on EITHER side unsupported by the wheel.. this is bad. this unsupported tire will flex and cause the car to be unstable at high speed and will TERRIBLY increase your slip angle causing your car to have to "settle" in a hard turn. I had 245-40-17 on my 8.5" wheels (rear) and they ROCKED. but they were on a WIDER wheel. a 225 is PLENTY. decent slip angle, predicitable handeling and with a decent tire you'll be hard pressed to break them loose anyway. I am currently running 205-60-14s on my stockys and although it doesn't "feel" as stable it will actually out handle my 17s. (me not driving) pulls over 1.1g on a skid pad... with me driving pulls .94 (then i tap the gas and i'm toast) Wider is better ONLY if you have the proper equipment. and a 245 on a 7" wheel isn't proper. it's dangerous. sorry for preaching... 245/25.4=9.64  9.64-7=2.64  2.64/2=1.32 (1.32" amount of unsupported tire on your 7" wheel per side.)||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||05-19-2000||11:35 PM||||oh decent tires I'd recommend   <P>Yokohama AVS intermediate  (on my Alfa 164)<BR>Yokohama A032r  (spare set for Fiero)<BR>Yokohama A008r<BR>Bridgestone RE71<BR>Pirelli P ZERO<BR>TOYO Proxes T1S<BR>NITTO NT555R<BR>Dunlop SP9000<BR>Dunlop SP8000<BR>BF Good-  any of their KD series<BR>Kumho 800<P>These are just a few of the stickey tires you can sort through for a sharp cornering car. a Falken although value engineered and just cause it fits will sacrifice handelin for ride quality. The Yokohama especially sacrifice ride quality for the road carving abilty.. i'm willing to live with that... and they WILL NOT last very long... (the Dunlop will) I replace the Alfa Tires about ever 8-10000 miles and thats streaching it. The A509s on my Fiero are not too flogged but with less than 6000 on them the wear indicator is about become much more pronounced and force me into a new set of intermediates. There is no real all in one tire... either all slung DEEP into a hard corner... or all stuck in traffic being comfy its your call.. but wider tires isn't the way to fix a handeling problem. They Do look nicer but once again you pick... all show or all go. and the 205-14s will hold PLENTY of power... I have wheel hop issues with both my 14s and my 17s. but the best numbers are ALWAYS on the 205s... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||2birds||05-20-2000||12:25 AM||||I have an 86 GT, stock 15x7 lace rims, lowered with Suspension Techniques springs.  I have 215/60/15 on the front, 225/60/15 on the back, Comp T/A HR4 all around.  The fronts will rub at full lock on the inner fender liner when brand new, with 40,000 miles they don't anymore.  The  fronts really fill the wheel opening, and almost stick out a litle too much.  The rear could be a half inch taller to fill the wheel opening a little better, maybe 235/60, but the 225/215 combo is real nice handling-wise, IMHO, and the speedo error is negligible.  I'll e-mail you a side view pic if you want, since I can't get the picture post thing to work (10% rule applies here, I think).  Let me know.||||reg||1
